§§ `Rule 2 -- powers of the Court`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:2919 | 2. —(1) Subject to any written law, costs are in the discretion of the Court and the Court has the power to determine all issues relating to the costs of or incidental to all proceedings in the Supreme Court or the State Courts at any stage of the proceedings or after the conclusion of the proceedings. (2) In exercising its power to fix or assess costs, the Court must have regard to all relevant circumstances, including — ( a )
@ref O 21 r 2(1) (src:2919-2919)
GIVEN `the proceedings are in the Supreme Court or the State Courts` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 21 r 2(1) costs are in the discretion of the Court` MEANS
    IF `the proceedings are in the Supreme Court or the State Courts`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "all proceedings in the Supreme Court or the State Courts"

DECLARE `o 21 r 2(2) circumstance` IS ONE OF
    `o 21 r 2(2)(a) efforts made by the parties at amicable resolution`
    `o 21 r 2(2)(b) the complexity of the case and the difficulty or novelty of the questions involved`
    `o 21 r 2(2)(c) the skill, specialised knowledge and responsibility required of, and the time and labour expended by, the solicitor`
    `o 21 r 2(2)(d) the urgency and importance of the action to the parties`
    `o 21 r 2(2)(e) the number of solicitors involved in the case for each party`
    `o 21 r 2(2)(f) the conduct of the parties`
    `o 21 r 2(2)(g) the principle of proportionality`
    `o 21 r 2(2)(h) the stage at which the proceedings were concluded`
    `o 21 r 2(2) another circumstance`

-- "In exercising its power to fix or assess costs, the Court must have regard to all relevant circumstances,
-- including": the eight listed are relevant; any other circumstance only if it is relevant.
-- src:2919 | 2. —(1) Subject to any written law, costs are in the discretion of the Court and the Court has the power to determine all issues relating to the costs of or incidental to all proceedings in the Supreme Court or the State Courts at any stage of the proceedings or after the conclusion of the proceedings. (2) In exercising its power to fix or assess costs, the Court must have regard to all relevant circumstances, including — ( a )
-- src:2920 | efforts made by the parties at amicable resolution;
-- src:2921 | ( b )
-- src:2922 | the complexity of the case and the difficulty or novelty of the questions involved;
-- src:2923 | ( c )
-- src:2924 | the skill, specialised knowledge and responsibility required of, and the time and labour expended by, the solicitor;
-- src:2925 | ( d )
-- src:2926 | the urgency and importance of the action to the parties;
-- src:2927 | ( e )
-- src:2928 | the number of solicitors involved in the case for each party;
-- src:2929 | ( f )
-- src:2930 | the conduct of the parties;
-- src:2931 | ( g )
-- src:2932 | the principle of proportionality; and
-- src:2933 | ( h )
